      <span class="tg-hero__type">Topical Study · Five Parts</span>
      <h1 class="tg-hero__title">Korah's Rebellion</h1>
      <p class="tg-hero__intro">
        Numbers 16–17 is the Bible's standing text on rebellion, priesthood, and who
        may draw near to God. It has been used for three thousand years to settle
        arguments — and, more often than the tradition admits, to end them. This study
        works through the text and its composition, the doctrines it opens, the divided
        church that has thrown it back and forth for five centuries, the answer the New
        Testament actually gives, and the way the chapter is used today against people
        who raise concerns about their leaders.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="tg-hero__key-verse">
        <p>All the congregation are holy, every one of them, and the LORD is among them;
           why then do you exalt yourselves above the assembly of the LORD?</p>
        <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 16:3">Numbers 16:3</a></cite>
      </blockquote>
    </div>
  </header><main>
    <div class="container">

      <section class="tg-section" id="why">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Why This Passage Is Difficult</h2>

        <p>Korah's charge is not heresy. It is a quotation. "All the congregation are
        holy" is <a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 19:6">Exodus 19:6</a> — "you shall be
        to me a kingdom of priests and a holy nation" — the covenant charter itself.
        The man swallowed by the earth was arguing from Scripture, and the Scripture he
        argued from is true.</p>

        <p>That is what makes the chapter generative and what makes it dangerous. It does
        not raise one doctrinal question; it raises the question that sits underneath a
        dozen others — <em>what kind of thing is holiness, and on what terms may a person
        approach God?</em> Nearly every downstream debate in the church, from ordination
        to schism to sacramental validity to the right to criticise a pastor, is an
        application of the answer given here.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Study in One Paragraph</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Korah wanted the answer <em>yes</em>. He got the
          answer <em>no</em>, and died. The church receives the answer <em>yes</em> — on
          entirely different grounds.</p>
          <p>Not "we are already holy," but "we have been made holy through the offering
          of the body of Jesus Christ once for all"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:10">Hebrews 10:10</a>).
          <strong>The conclusion Korah wanted is Christian doctrine; the argument he used
          remains false.</strong> The mechanism is the whole difference — and it is
          almost never said from the pulpit.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="parts">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Five Parts</h2>

        <p>Each part stands on its own and can be read in any order, though they build.
        Parts I–III are diagnostic; Part IV is constructive; Part V is pastoral.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Part I</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading"><a href="part-1.html">Text, Reception, Division Lines, and the Weight of Evidence</a></div>
          <p>The coalition and its two distinct grievances; the two judgments; the
          structural spine through <a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 18">Numbers 18</a>;
          the textual cruxes. Then the composition debate stated at its strongest on both
          sides, the ancient Jewish and patristic reception, the Reformation quarrel and
          its inversion — and an assessment of what the passage will and will not bear.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Part II</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading"><a href="part-2.html">The Theological Sprawl: Where Numbers 16 Goes</a></div>
          <p>Christ as the anti-Korah; priesthood, office and call; schism and the
          Donatist question; the sanctified censers; election and the mixed body;
          corporate solidarity and the children; intercession; the discernment problem
          (Korah or Jeremiah?); political theology; the budding rod; and the Korahite
          psalter — the canon's own answer, which the polemical tradition ignored.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Part III</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading"><a href="part-3.html">Standing Somewhere: Placement, Assurance, and the Other Side</a></div>
          <p>Each Western communion's account of itself in the strongest form its own
          best theologians would recognise, with the Orthodox East as the third party.
          Where the disagreement is real, where it is not, what the Joint Declaration did
          and did not do — and the personal question people actually ask at two in the
          morning.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Part IV</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading"><a href="part-4.html">The Answer Given: Church, Community, Mission, Message, and Works</a></div>
          <p>The turn from diagnosis to construction. Korah asked a question; the New
          Testament answers it and builds an ecclesiology, a mission, and an ethic on the
          answer. The boundary removed and the direction reversed; the priesthood of all
          believers and what its sacrifices actually are; Matthew 18 and Acts 6 as the
          procedure Korah inverted; the fire that consumes the work and spares the worker.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Part V</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading"><a href="part-5.html">Spiritual Abuse: Korah, Jeremiah, and the Use of a Text as a Muzzle</a></div>
          <p>The passage's most common real-world application, and the one the
          commentaries handle least. What the canon says about leaders who harm the
          people they lead; the companion proof-texts dismantled; a seven-question test
          for telling Korah from Jeremiah; when it is a crime; when the charge runs the
          other way; and what the Psalter — written by Korah's own sons — says to the
          people this chapter has been used against.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="method">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">A Note on Method</h2>

        <p>This study tries to do three things consistently. It states each position in
        the form its own best advocates would recognise, rather than the form easiest to
        refute. It marks the difference between what is well supported, what is genuinely
        contested, and what is weak — including where the weak link is an argument the
        study's own tradition depends on. And it names tensions the tradition has never
        resolved rather than papering over them.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Two Findings That Shape Everything Below</span>
          <strong>First</strong>, the passage cannot settle an ecclesiological dispute,
          because the analogy that activates it — <em>Aaronide priest is to Korah as our
          ministers are to their critics</em> — is itself the thing in dispute. Both
          sides of every division since 1 Clement have used it, and the four-century
          inversion war after the Reformation is not an accident.
          <strong>Second</strong>, Numbers 16 stipulates a <em>righteous</em> Moses:
          every charge brought against him is false, and the narrative proves it. The
          chapter therefore adjudicates rebellion against legitimate and faithful
          authority. It contains no ruling whatever on resistance to abusive authority,
          because that case does not arise in it.
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="preaching">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Six Controls for Using This Passage</h2>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">For Teachers and Preachers</span>
          <p><strong>1. Preach both signs.</strong> Fire and the pit — <em>and</em> the
          budding rod. God answered the same challenge twice, once by death and once by
          life, and the sign kept in the ark is the living one.</p>
          <p><strong>2. Never stop at Numbers 17.</strong>
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 26:11">Numbers 26:11</a> and the Korahite
          psalms are canonical, and they change the passage's shape.</p>
          <p><strong>3. Name the equivocation, not just the sin.</strong> Korah's premise
          was Exodus 19:6. What failed was the inference. A hearer who has never quoted
          Scripture in service of his own ambition is rare.</p>
          <p><strong>4. Watch the direction of the accusation.</strong> Moses calls no one
          Korah. He falls on his face and defers the verdict to God. Any application in
          which the leader identifies the Korahs is already outside the text's pattern.</p>
          <p><strong>5. Preach the mediator, not the hierarchy.</strong> Aaron standing
          between the dead and the living with a censer is where this passage is going.
          The one who defends his office is not in the story.</p>
          <p><strong>6. Land on Hebrews 10.</strong> Korah's question — <em>may I draw
          near?</em> — receives a canonical <em>yes</em>. Not on his terms. On better ones.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

